<%/* The letterhead — our identity, the customer's, and the logo between them.
     ONE partial rather than three because the DOM says so: `#logo`, `#bill-to`
     and `#cfs` are three siblings of `#grid-wrapper` in that order, and
     `#bill-to` sits BETWEEN the two blocks that are purely ours. Splitting them
     would mean three includes to reproduce one contiguous run of markup.

     Props:
       title            the document's own heading — "Quote #123", "Invoice #2390"
       customer_name    the customer's name, ALREADY COMPOSED, or null
       billing_address  the customer's billing address, or null

     🔴 **It takes a NAME, not an `organization`, and the reason CHANGED with
     core#93 — the old one is no longer true.** It used to be that a movement
     session carried `{uid, name}` and NO chain, so composing here would throw on
     a receipt. Every org-bearing source now carries a chain, so that argument is
     void.

     What survives is narrower and still decides it: the two populations spell
     the chain under DIFFERENT KEYS. An order and an invoice carry a frozen
     `DocumentOrganizationSnapshot` at `organization.path`; a pick sheet and a
     movement session carry `organization.organization_path`. So there is still
     no single expression this partial could evaluate that is right for both, and
     each caller composes with `it.organizations.composeOrgName` before handing
     the result over — which is also what keeps the two cases legible at the call
     site.

     ⚠️ `customer_name` may be NULL, and null is an ANSWER rather than missing
     data. `POST /returns` deliberately accepts bookings from different orders —
     so a session spanning two customers is legitimate, and `agreedOrganization`
     returns null to say so. A receipt must not print a customer name in that
     case; collapsing to the first would put one customer's name on another's
     goods. An order and an invoice always carry one, so this guard changes
     nothing they render.

     ⚠️ `billing_address` may be null too, and independently: a session never has
     one, and `billing_address` is `.optional()` on the organization snapshot
     while `Address` is itself `.nullable()`, so an order can legitimately carry
     neither (#25).

     `it.logo` is inherited, not passed: it is injected into every render
     context by `renderTemplate` and is not a fact about the document. */%>
<%
const { title, customer_name, billing_address } = it;
%>
  <div id="logo">
    <%~ it.logo %>
  </div>
  <section id="bill-to">
    <%
    // The lines are assembled rather than emitted as bare spans because a
    // separator only belongs there when both sides of it are present.
    // `street2` defaults to `""`, so the old markup printed a leading comma on
    // every order without a suite number.
    const billing = billing_address ?? {};
    const streetLine = [billing.street, billing.street2].filter(Boolean).join(", ");
    const cityLine = [
      [billing.city, billing.region].filter(Boolean).join(", "),
      billing.postcode,
    ].filter(Boolean).join(" ");
    // `country_name` is stored with a space. Matching "UnitedStates" never
    // matched anything, so the country line printed on every domestic quote.
    const foreign = billing.country_name && billing.country_name !== "United States";
    %>
    <h1><%= title %></h1>
    <% if (customer_name) { %>
    <div><%= customer_name %></div>
    <% } %>
    <% if (streetLine) { %>
    <div><%= streetLine %></div>
    <% } %>
    <% if (cityLine) { %>
    <div><%= cityLine %></div>
    <% } %>
    <% if (foreign) { %>
    <div><%= billing.country_name %></div>
    <% } %>
  </section>
  <section id="cfs">
    <h1>Chicago Film Supplies</h1>
    <div>3100 W Fillmore St, Chicago, IL 60612</div>
    <div>team@chicagofilmsupplies.com</div>
    <div>312.818.3008</div>
  </section>
